2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the Regional Government establishes action programs and activities for protection of women and children from acts of violence in 1 (one) RAD as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children who become victims of acts of violence.
3. RAD as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
4. RAD as referred to in paragraph (2) is further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 13

1. Forms of community participation in protecting women and children from acts of violence, include:
a. forming Family Partners at the kelurahan level;
b. forming Women and Children Protection Units within community organizations;
c. conducting socialization of women's and children's rights independently;
d. providing first aid to women and children who become victims of acts of violence; ande. reporting to the competent authority if violence occurs in their environment against women and children who become victims of acts of violence.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector and mass media.
3. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 16

1. Implementation of protection for women from acts of violence is carried out through activities:a. prevention of acts of violence;
b. assistance and recovery services for women who become victims of acts of violence;
c. reunification; andd. empowerment of women who become victims of acts of violence.
2. Implementation of protection of children from acts of violence is carried out through activities:a. prevention of acts of violence;
b. handling services for children who become victims of acts of violence;
c. reunification; andd. rehabilitation of children who become victims of acts of violence.
3. Implementation of protection as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is carried out by the Regional Government through the Agency.

Article 2

The scope of this regional regulation includes: a. acts of violence;
b. implementation of protection;
c. implementation of prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
d. implementation of services, assistance and recovery for women and children who become victims of acts of violence;
e. institutions;
f. reunification;
g. empowerment and rehabilitation;
h. guidance and supervision; andi. financing.

Article 34

Forms of PPT services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence, include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of women and children who are victims of acts of violence carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services to assist women and children who are victims of acts of violence in undergoing legal processes; and e. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that women and children who are victims of acts of violence can be independent.

Article 28

1. Services for assistance and recovery of women and children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 25 are provided to women and children who are domiciled in the Region and who experience violence within the Region.
2. Forms of assistance and recovery services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in the form of:a. women's empowerment;
b. child rehabilitation;
c. complaint, consultation and counseling services; and d. assistance services.
3. Provisions regarding the form and procedures for assistance and recovery services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence shall be further regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 9

Neglect as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (2) letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the unfulfillment of the child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social carried out by parents, guardians or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for or take care of the child as they should carried out by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect the wife within the scope of her household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract, he is obliged to provide livelihood, care or maintenance to his wife; and/ord. acts that result in economic dependence, by restricting and/or prohibiting the wife from working properly inside or outside the home so that the wife is under the control of her husband.

Article 25

1. Every woman and child who becomes a victim of acts of violence has the right to receive:a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and g. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and community member.
2. The Regional Government guarantees the fulfillment of the rights of women and children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1).

Article 23

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women is carried out through activities:a. formation of networks;
b. implementation of coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community, including the private sector and community organizations; and c. socialization of laws and regulations governing the protection of women.
2. Prevention of acts of violence against children is carried out through activities:a. formation of networks;
b. implementation of coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community including the private sector and existing community organizations;
c. socialization of laws and regulations governing child protection; and d. education for parents.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is carried out by involving:a. families;
b. communities;
c. private sector;
d. community organizations; and e. educational institutions.

